"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[7247],{7247:n=>{n.exports="# Transactions & Locking\n\nSource dialect transaction and locking patterns → DBSQL equivalents and unsupported features.\n\n> For DBSQL MERGE syntax, load `skills/writing-sql/data-modification.md`.\n\n## MSSQL\n\n### Transactions\n\n**Critical limitations:**\n\n1. **No nested transactions** — `BEGIN TRANSACTION` inside an active transaction raises an error\n2. **No savepoints** — T-SQL `SAVE TRANSACTION` has no equivalent\n3. **No DDL inside transactions** — Cannot run `CREATE`, `ALTER`, or `DROP` inside a `BEGIN TRANSACTION` / `COMMIT` block. Move DDL statements outside the transaction.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL: CREATE TABLE #temp inside a transaction (common pattern)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    CREATE TABLE #staging (id INT, val VARCHAR(50));\n    INSERT INTO #staging SELECT id, val FROM source;\n    MERGE INTO target USING #staging ON ...;\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL: create the temp table/view before BEGIN TRANSACTION\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW staging AS SELECT id, val FROM source;\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    MERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target USING staging ON ...;\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\n**Runtime note:** `BEGIN ATOMIC` requires Databricks SQL Serverless or DBR 14.0+. On earlier runtimes, use `BEGIN TRANSACTION ... COMMIT` instead.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT TRANSACTION;\n\n-- DBSQL (interactive)\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nCOMMIT;\n\n-- DBSQL (recommended: ATOMIC blocks with auto-rollback)\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\n### Nested transactions (unsupported)\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL nested transactions\nBEGIN TRANSACTION outer;\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    BEGIN TRANSACTION inner;  -- T-SQL tracks nesting; final COMMIT only commits when @@TRANCOUNT = 0\n        INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\n    COMMIT TRANSACTION inner;\nCOMMIT TRANSACTION outer;\n\n-- DBSQL: flatten into a single ATOMIC block\nBEGIN ATOMIC\n    INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (1);\n    INSERT INTO t2 VALUES (2);\nEND;\n```\n\n### SAVEPOINT (unsupported)\n\nT-SQL `SAVE TRANSACTION name` / `ROLLBACK TRANSACTION name` → No DBSQL equivalent. Design workaround: separate ATOMIC blocks, or track state in a staging table.\n\n### OUTPUT clause / OUTPUT INTO\n\nT-SQL `OUTPUT` clause on INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE captures affected rows. DBSQL has no direct equivalent.\n\n**INSERT with OUTPUT:**\n\nThere is no direct equivalent. The two approaches are:\n\n1. **Separate INSERT + SELECT** — insert the row, then query it back using the known key. Fragile under concurrent writes; only use when the table has a deterministic unique key you control.\n2. **MERGE with explicit tracking** — preferred. Write a source-side staging table and MERGE, then capture what changed by joining target to source on the key. See `data-modification.md` for MERGE syntax.\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nINSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount)\nOUTPUT inserted.order_id, inserted.amount INTO #audit\nVALUES (1, 100.00);\n\n-- DBSQL option 1: insert then re-select by known key (only when key is deterministic)\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (order_id, customer_id, amount) VALUES (42, 1, 100.00);\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit (order_id, amount)\nSELECT order_id, amount FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders WHERE order_id = 42;\n\n-- DBSQL option 2: MERGE approach (preferred — no race condition)\n-- Load source_catalog.my_schema.new_orders with the rows to insert, then:\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.new_orders s ON t.order_id = s.order_id\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (order_id, customer_id, amount) VALUES (s.order_id, s.customer_id, s.amount);\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit (order_id, amount)\nSELECT s.order_id, s.amount FROM my_catalog.my_schema.new_orders s;\n```\n\n**MERGE with OUTPUT:**\n\n```sql\n-- T-SQL\nMERGE INTO target AS t\nUSING source AS s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET t.val = s.val\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (id, val) VALUES (s.id, s.val)\nOUTPUT $action, inserted.id INTO audit_log;\n\n-- DBSQL: capture changes via separate log INSERT (Unity Catalog Delta tables)\nMERGE INTO my_catalog.my_schema.target AS t\nUSING my_catalog.my_schema.source AS s ON t.id = s.id\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET val = s.val\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (id, val) VALUES (s.id, s.val);\n-- Capture what changed by joining target to source after the merge completes:\nINSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.audit_log\nSELECT 'UPSERT', t.id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.target t\nINNER JOIN my_catalog.my_schema.source s ON t.id = s.id;\n-- Note: this captures the post-merge state of matched rows, not a per-row action log.\n-- Unlike T-SQL OUTPUT which records $action ('INSERT' vs 'UPDATE') per row, this approach\n-- cannot distinguish inserted rows from updated rows without additional bookkeeping.\n```\n\n## SYNAPSE\n\nSame as MSSQL. Additional note: Synapse supports `BEGIN DISTRIBUTED TRANSACTION` (MSDTC) — no equivalent in DBSQL.\n"}}]);
